    "q-332244": {
      "dom_id": "step-q332244",
      "name": "Question 5",
      "problem": "A data set contains the observations ${x}_{1}=3.1,{x}_{2}=7.9$ with $z$-scores ${z}_{1}=-0.5$ and ${z}_{2}=1.5,$ respectively. Find the standard deviation $\\sigma $ of the data set.\n\n$\\sigma =$\n{{field-1}}",
      "worked_solution": "To compute the value of an observation $x$ which yields a given $z$-score in a data set with mean $\\overset{―}{x}$ and standard deviation $\\sigma ,$ we can use the following formula:\n\n$$\nx=\\overset{―}{x}+z\\cdot \\sigma \n$$\n\nFrom the first observation, we have\n\n$$\n3.1=\\overset{¯}{x}+(-0.5)\\cdot \\sigma .\n$$\n\nFrom the second observation, we have\n\n$$\n7.9=\\overset{¯}{x}+1.5\\cdot \\sigma .\n$$\n\nSubtracting the first equation from the second and solving for $\\sigma ,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}7.9-3.1 & =(1.5-(-0.5))\\cdot \\sigma  \\\\ 4.8 & =2\\cdot \\sigma  \\\\ \\sigma  & =2.4.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
